cal Polar Coordinates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129389 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186130207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129389 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129389 #edb/ref 1050 960.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129389 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "82aa6e24-e2d2-572b-b1aa-bfcb7c8c2948" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129516 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129516 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161520" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129516 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129516 #edb/ref 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25$  for $x\\ge 0$ between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=6+y.$\nExpress the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}xz\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129516 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1a6db462-1cdd-5328-ac3f-82ce0a5c5a14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129516 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b1/b194b4882dcada56a10ec69f9dae80b363ea05efaae27385ceacc9a15ba3b56f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $5$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=6+y=6+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z\\le 6+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}xz\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )z\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{6+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129517 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129517 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161521" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129517 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129517 #edb/ref 1071 "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}\\le 9,\\,0\\le z\\le 2+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}xy\\,\\text{d}V=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129517 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0593d504-dbf8-59c9-9282-2775ebdf4b88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129517 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/75/75bec31176c369e69d5b11af3eeab23f86a4f32251e37f56b225e9dd0bd4a3de.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=2+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2+{r}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +9 & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le 2+{r}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}xy\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{2+{r}^{2}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129767 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129767 #edb/ref 1069 "q-40785" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129767 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129767 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2z\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$  for $y\\ge 0$ and between the surfaces $z=-x$ and $z=2+x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129767 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c05e88d4-386c-59e5-8052-d6f2602a779c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129767 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/06/063fafc4f8c50dba56bcfb50b8366a2ffb8c713f5668ce22d10c0370376bbaba.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-x=-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=2+x=2+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le z\\le 2+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2z\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}2z\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{2+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }2rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r[{z}^{2}]_{z=-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{z=2+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r((2+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}4r+4{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{[2{r}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]}_{r=0}^{r=3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2(3)^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}(3)^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }18+36\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[18\\theta +36\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =\\pi } \\\\  & =18(\\pi -0)+36(0-0) \\\\  & =18\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130207 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130207 #edb/ref 1061 42893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130207 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8d1c0646-172c-5530-a344-03c704adfee5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130207 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130208 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130208 #edb/ref 1061 42894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130208 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4052d2ac-00ee-5541-8083-57bb342065db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130208 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130209 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130209 #edb/ref 1061 42895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130209 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "04baaba8-4f7b-509d-81e6-12d1b9edece3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130209 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130222 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130222 #edb/ref 1061 44539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130222 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b4729478-340f-5423-b9c8-e2511cd7d6c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130222 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130237 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130237 #edb/ref 1061 44790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130237 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c603c442-b1a4-5adf-ae0d-730bdeda4732" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130237 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130251 #edb/ref 1061 45005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130251 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e1410a08-b410-5156-bc97-92666f25c17f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130251 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130456 #edb/ref 1064 "We can evaluate triple integrals by changing the variables to cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R x^2 + y^2 \\:\\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the finite region enclosed between the surfaces $z =0$ and $z = 1 - x^2 - y^2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a63e349d2776fe07077379b9d603e32ad2ff264cfa8005a226422b44f7e100fe.png)\n\n \n\nThis is a challenging integral to evaluate as it currently stands. However, we should recognize that the problem naturally lends itself to cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\nRecall that if $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b2/b2f8a6fe7f21b062eec294522e411c1e471b564eae68763d4767f917d65b54b0.png)\n\nNotice that the region $R$ is\n\n- bounded below by $z = 0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z = 1 - x^2 - y^2 = 1 - r^2.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta,z) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1-r^2 \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/da/dae76df462a695e655ffdde01559d19bf245aa6d601100fb8c7bc1024106a4c8.png)\n\n \n\nTo express our integral in terms of cylindrical polar coordinates, we will use the change of variables formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, {\\color{blue}\\mathrm{d}V} = \n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta, z) \\:  {\\color{blue}r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta}.\n$$\n\nWhen expressing our integral in cylindrical polar coordinates, we must write \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{\\textrm{d}V}} = {\\color{blue}r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta}.\n$$\n\nIt's important not to forget the extra factor of ${\\color{blue}{r}}.$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{r}^{2}}{r}^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} \\int_{0}^{\\bbox[2pt,white]{\\color{black}1}} \\int_{0}^{\\bbox[2pt,white]{\\color{black}1 - r^2 }} \\bbox[2pt,white]{\\color{black} r^3} \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}r \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta\n=\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R x^2 + y^2 \\:\\textrm{d}V = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130456 #edb/ref 1065 7193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130456 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c6d9a5c4-532b-53e1-9689-7b5dbc30ec98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130456 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130470 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now build some intuition behind the meaning of the volume element $\\textrm d V.$ This will help us to understand the change of variables formula in cylindrical polar coordinates. We start by considering the usual rectangular coordinate system.\n\nSuppose we have a point $P$ and a neighboring point $Q$ in the Cartesian $xyz$-space, as shown below. \n\n![](images/25/2535744585beb7fed89d760c0290a04f54d0a08c8f7e564b17c8e42432da031e.png)\n\n \n\nImagine moving from $P$ to $Q$ as follows:\n\n- First, we move a distance $\\textrm d x$ parallel to the $x$-axis so that the new point $P'$ has the same $x$-coordinate as $Q.$\n\n- Then, we move a distance $\\textrm d y$ parallel to the $y$-axis so that the new point $P''$ has the same $y$-coordinate as $Q.$\n\n- Finally, we move a distance $\\textrm d z$ parallel to the $z$-axis to land to $Q.$\n\nThese movements span the rectangular solid shown above. The volume of this solid, denoted $\\textrm d V,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{d}V = \\mathrm{d}x \\, \\mathrm{d}y \\, \\mathrm{d}z.\n$$\n\nThus, the element $\\textrm d V$ represents the volume generated by moving from the point $P$ to the point $Q$ in this coordinate system." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130470 #edb/ref 1065 7731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130470 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "82dc2175-bfda-5b86-bba2-acb36d6cd7ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130470 #edb/ref 1067 "Deriving the Volume Element in Cartesian Coordinates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130473 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now repeat this process for a cylindrical polar system. Consider the points $P$ and $Q$ shown below.\n\n![](images/24/243d720546357a33eac3f96b84755cf6aeebafdef3a3672a3d29989147f134c2.png)\n\n \n\nImagine moving from $P$ to $Q$ as follows:\n\n- First, we move a distance $\\textrm d r$ so that the new point $P'$ has the same $r$-coordinate as $Q.$\n\n- Then, we sweep through an angle $\\textrm d \\theta$ so that the new point $P''$ has the same $\\theta$-coordinate as $Q.$ The arc length traced out by this movement approximately equals $r\\,\\textrm d \\theta.$\n\n- Finally, we move a distance $\\textrm d z$ vertically to land on $Q.$\n\nThese three movements trace out a solid that's approximately rectangular, and so the approximate volume of this solid is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{d}V = \\mathrm{d}r \\cdot r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\cdot \\mathrm{d}z=  r\\,\\mathrm{d}z\\,\\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130473 #edb/ref 1065 7772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130473 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0a132073-0472-5027-b85c-8d1bed6673d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130473 #edb/ref 1067 "Deriving the Volume Element in Cylindrical Polar Coordinates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367666 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186367667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367666 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161607" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367666 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367666 #edb/ref 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2$ between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=\\sqrt{16-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+z}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367666 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0602a248-c988-5793-8ea6-c87cceac7390" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367666 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/36/36839d1fbde65e86df7335d40374fe72a5ff43ed92b89405327406a62dc8763c.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{16-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+z}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{1+z}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 